  "pContainerHelp": "The container is the \"box\" holding the tracks. MP4 plays everywhere (phones, browsers, messengers). MKV accepts almost any codec and subtitles; good for archiving. MOV is for Apple and editing. WebM is for the web (VP9/AV1 + Opus). Audio only: MP3, M4A, Opus, FLAC, WAV. Default: MP4.",
  "pVideoCodec": "Video codec",
  "pVideoCodecHint": "How the picture is compressed",
  "pVideoCodecHelp": "H.264: maximum compatibility. H.265: 30–50% smaller at the same quality but not supported everywhere. AV1: smaller still, slower to encode (fast on new GPUs). VP9: for WebM. ProRes and DNxHR: for editing (large files). FFV1: lossless. \"Copy\": instant and lossless if the container fits. Default: H.264.",
  "pAudioCodec": "Audio codec",
  "pAudioCodecHint": "How the audio is compressed",
  "pHardware": "Hardware encoding",
  "pHardwareOn": "Using the GPU: {name}",
  "pHardwareOff": "Encoding on the CPU: {name}",
  "pHardwareHelp": "The GPU (NVENC, Quick Sync, AMF, VAAPI) encodes 5–20× faster than the CPU, but quality per file size is slightly lower. Turn on for quick jobs, off for archiving and smallest size. Without a suitable GPU the CPU is used. Default: on.",
  "pEncoder": "Encoder",
  "pEncoderHint": "A specific implementation from the ffmpeg build",
  "pRateMode": "Mode",
  "pRateModeHint": "How size and quality are controlled",
  "pRateModeHelp": "\"Quality\" (CRF/CQ) is nearly always best: constant quality, size adapts to scene complexity. \"Bitrate\": average kb/s, predictable size. \"Constant\": the same rate throughout, for streaming and old players. \"File size\": enter the megabytes you need (e.g. a messenger limit); bitrate is computed, two-pass when possible. Default: Quality.",
  "pQuality": "Quality ({scale})",
  "pQualityHint": "Lower number: better quality, bigger file",
  "pQualityHelp": "This is the encoder's \"constant quality\": every scene is encoded to look equally good. A change of 6 roughly doubles or halves the size. For x264: 18 is visually lossless, 23 is standard, 28 is noticeably worse. AV1/VP9 use a scale up to 63. Default for this encoder: {value}.",
  "pVideoBitrate": "Video bitrate",
  "pVideoBitrateHint": "Kilobits per second for video",
  "pVideoBitrateHelp": "H.264 guide: 1080p 4000–8000, 720p 2500–5000, 480p 1000–2000 kb/s. H.265/AV1 need about 30–50% less.",
  "pTargetSize": "File size",
  "pTargetSizeHint": "The result will not exceed this",
  "pTargetSizeHelp": "The bitrate is computed from duration: (size × 8) / seconds − audio, with 3% headroom for the container. Too small a size for a long video looks blurry; lower the resolution. Discord: 10 MB (use 9.5), Telegram bots: 50 MB.",
  "pTwoPass": "Two-pass",
  "pTwoPassHint": "Hits the size more precisely, takes twice as long",
  "pTwoPassHelp": "The first pass analyzes the video, the second distributes bits: more to complex scenes, less to simple ones. Best quality at a given size. Not needed in Quality mode. Default: on.",
  "pSpeed": "Encoding speed",
  "pSpeedHint": "Slower: smaller file at the same quality",
  "pSpeedHelp": "The encoder speed preset (x264 veryfast…veryslow, NVENC p1…p7, SVT-AV1 12…4). Quality is set separately; a slower preset just compresses better. \"Medium\" is a good balance; \"slow\" saves 5–10% for 2–3× the time. Default: medium.",
  "pResolution": "Resolution",
  "pResolutionHint": "Downscale to this height (never upscales)",
  "pResolutionHelp": "The aspect ratio is kept and the width rounded to even. Lowering the resolution is the most effective way to shrink: 1080p → 720p roughly halves the file. Default: keep original.",
  "pFps": "Frame rate",
  "pFpsHint": "Frames per second",
  "pTenBit": "10-bit",
  "pTenBitHint": "Less banding on gradients, needed for HDR",
  "pTenBitHelp": "10 bits per channel remove banding in skies and shadows and slightly improve H.265/AV1 compression. Old devices and some browsers cannot play 10-bit H.264. Default: off.",
  "pProfile": "Profile",
  "pProfileHint": "Codec compatibility constraints",
  "pIntermediateProfileHint": "Quality and size of the editing format",
  "pIntermediateProfileHelp": "ProRes: proxy < lt < standard < hq < 4444 < 4444xq. DNxHR: lb < sq < hq < hqx (10-bit) < 444. HQ is usually enough for editing. Default: HQ.",
  "pTune": "Tune",
  "pTuneHint": "Optimize for the type of content",
  "pTuneHelp": "film: live action, animation: cartoons, grain: keep film grain, stillimage: slideshows, zerolatency: low-latency streaming. Default: none.",
  "pKeyint": "Keyframe interval",
  "pKeyintHint": "How often playback can start from any point",
  "pKeyintHelp": "More frequent keyframes mean faster seeking and more precise fast trims, but larger files. YouTube and streaming recommend 2 seconds. Default: auto (encoder decides).",
  "pAudioBitrate": "Audio bitrate",
  "pAudioBitrateHint": "Higher: better sound, bigger file",
  "pAudioBitrateHelp": "Guide: AAC 128 is fine for speech and music, 192–256 for music, 320 is the maximum. Opus already sounds good at 96. MP3 320 is the format limit. Default: depends on the codec.",
  "pChannels": "Channels",
  "pChannelsHint": "E.g. downmix 5.1 to stereo",
  "pSampleRate": "Sample rate",
  "pSampleRateHint": "48 kHz for video, 44.1 kHz for music",
  "pFaststart": "Fast start",
  "pFaststartHint": "Starts playing in a browser before fully downloaded",
  "pFaststartHelp": "Moves the index (moov) to the start of MP4/MOV. Needed for websites and messengers, costs nothing. Default: on.",
  "pKeepSubs": "Keep subtitles",
  "pKeepSubsHint": "Copy subtitle tracks as is",
  "pKeepMetadata": "Keep metadata",
  "pKeepMetadataHint": "Tags, chapters, capture date. Turn off to strip them",
  "pTrimMode": "Trim method",
  "trimFast": "Fast",
  "trimPrecise": "Frame-accurate",
  "trimFastHint": "Instant and lossless; starts at the nearest keyframe",
  "trimPreciseHint": "Re-encodes: exact boundaries, takes longer",
  "pTrimModeHelp": "Video is stored in groups of frames that start with a keyframe. Fast trimming copies data as is, so it may start slightly before your point, at the nearest keyframe (usually 0–5 s). Frame-accurate re-encodes the fragment and cuts exactly; quality is barely affected.",
